<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-factors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Factors</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-factors">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-data-shapes" /> argued that shape is not only rows and
      columns; it includes what an observation and a variable actually
      represent. A <term>factor</term><idx><h>factor</h></idx> is R's way of
      representing a categorical variable: a fixed, named set of
      <term>levels</term> that a variable is allowed to take. It is easy to
      treat a factor as a purely technical device <mdash /> a column of
      labels, nothing more. The story below is about a case where the
      labels were never neutral, where they were imposed by a state on an
      entire population, where they changed meaning over time, and where
      that change makes a seemingly simple comparison genuinely difficult
      to do correctly.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is real history, and it is treated as such here: not as a
      convenient or dramatic data-wrangling puzzle, but as a case where a
      system of classification materially shaped where people could live,
      work, and belong, and whose consequences the country is still
      accounting for in its own statistics today.
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: When a category changes the data                      -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-population-group-factors">
    <title>When a Category Changes the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Imagine two datasets describing the population of South Africa.
        One was collected in 1980, under apartheid. The other was collected
        in 2022, nearly three decades after apartheid ended. Both contain a
        variable called <c>population_group</c>. You might reasonably think
        you could compare them directly. You cannot, and the reason why is
        the subject of this chapter.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-legal-classification">
      <title>A classification imposed by law</title>

      <p>
        From 1950, every person in South Africa was legally required to be
        classified into a racial category under the Population Registration
        Act (Act No. 30 of 1950). The Act established four main categories
        <mdash /> White, Coloured, Native (later <q>Bantu</q>), and, once
        added, Asian <mdash /> and this classification became the
        administrative foundation that later apartheid laws were built on
        and enforced through, including the Group Areas Act, which
        determined where a person of each classification was permitted to
        live, and the Bantu Authorities Act, which restructured governance
        for the population classified as Native. By 1959, under Proclamation
        No. 46, the Coloured category itself had been subdivided further
        still: Cape Coloured, Cape Malay, Griqua, Chinese, Indian, Other
        Asiatic, and Other Coloured, distinguished using criteria that
        included physical appearance and the area a person lived in.
      </p>

      <p>
        This was not a system for describing a population as researchers
        might observe it. It was a system for sorting people into groups
        that the state then treated differently under the law, and the
        people being classified generally had no say in which category they
        were assigned to.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-1980-census">
      <title>What the 1980 census actually recorded</title>

      <p>
        The categories used in practice were far more detailed than <q>four
        main groups</q> suggests. The population-group variable in South
        Africa's 1980 census codebook lists, among its coded values: White;
        Cape Coloured, Cape Malay, Griqua, and Other Coloured; Chinese,
        Indian, and Other Asian; and, for the population classified as
        Black, separate codes for Xhosa, Zulu, Swazi, South Ndebele, North
        Ndebele, North Sotho, South Sotho, Tswana, Shangaan/Tsonga, Venda,
        Lemba, and Other Blacks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read as a factor, <c>population_group</c> in 1980 did not have four
        levels. It had at least twenty, encoding not just <q>race</q> as
        the Act defined it, but ethnic and linguistic subdivisions within
        the category the state classified as Black, subdivisions that
        served the administrative logic of apartheid <mdash /> including,
        eventually, the assignment of people to <q>homelands</q> along
        ethnic lines.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-self-classification">
      <title>The law was repealed. The variable didn't disappear.</title>

      <p>
        The Population Registration Act was repealed by the Population
        Registration Act Repeal Act (Act No. 114 of 1991), which ended
        legal racial classification in South Africa from June 1991.
        Statistics South Africa did not stop collecting population-group
        data after apartheid ended. What changed was the basis on which it
        was collected: from the 1996 census onward, population group has
        been <term>self-identified</term> <mdash /> a respondent's own
        answer <mdash /> rather than a classification imposed under law.
        The 1996 census recorded self-reported population groups of
        Black/African (77%), White (11%), Coloured (9%), Asian/Indian
        (2.6%), and Other (0.9%).
      </p>

      <p>
        By Census 2022, Statistics South Africa's published results used
        five categories: Black African (81.4% of the population, some
        50.5 million people), Coloured (8.2%), White (7.3%, roughly 4.5
        million people, down from 4.59 million in 2011), Indian/Asian
        (2.7%), and Other.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-the-problem">
      <title>Which old category maps to which new one?</title>

      <p>
        Now suppose you actually want to compare 1980 and 2022: how has the
        population in each group changed over four decades? You cannot
        write <c>population ~ population_group + year</c> and treat
        <c>population_group</c> as if it means the same thing in both
        years, because it does not.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some of the mapping is reasonably direct: the categories legally
        defined as White in 1980 and self-identified as White in 2022 refer
        to broadly the same group, collected in a different way. But <q>Cape
        Malay</q> and <q>Griqua</q>, coded separately in 1980 as
        subdivisions of Coloured, do not appear at all in the 2022
        categories; they are presumably folded into <q>Coloured</q>, but
        the 2022 data alone cannot tell you that, and no amount of
        relabelling factor levels in R can recover a distinction the newer
        collection method never asked about. In the other direction, the
        nine or more separate ethnic codes recorded within the 1980
        <q>Black</q> classification <mdash /> Xhosa, Zulu, Tswana, Venda,
        and the rest <mdash /> collapse into a single <q>Black African</q>
        category in 2022. Going from 1980 to 2022, you can aggregate levels
        together with reasonable confidence. Going from 2022 back to 1980,
        the information needed to disaggregate simply is not there.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-factors-not-neutral">
        <title>A factor level is not a neutral label</title>
        <p>
          The levels of <c>population_group</c> in 1980 were not a smaller,
          less accurate version of the levels used in 2022. They were
          produced by an entirely different process, with a different
          purpose, under a different legal and political system, asking a
          different question of the people being counted <mdash /> a
          question, in 1980, that people did not get to answer for
          themselves. Fixing a comparison like this is not a matter of
          renaming <c>Bantu</c> to <c>Black African</c> and moving on. It
          requires knowing what each classification system was built to do,
          who defined its categories, and what changed between one system
          and the next <mdash /> and even then, some of what changed cannot
          be recovered from the data alone.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        A factor, in other words, is not just R syntax for a column of
        text. It represents a classification system that someone, at some
        point, chose to impose on the world. Sometimes, as here, that
        choice was made under a legal system built specifically to divide
        people unequally. Understanding a factor's levels means
        understanding that choice, not just typing <c>levels()</c> and
        reading off whatever comes back.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Population Registration Act, 1950 (Act No. 30
        of 1950), and Population Registration Act Repeal Act, 1991 (Act No.
        114 of 1991), Parliament of South Africa. South Africa Population
        Census 1980, population-group variable codebook, World Bank
        Microdata Library. Khalfani, A.A. and Zuberi, T. (2001). <q>Racial
        classification and the modern census in South Africa,
        1911<ndash />1996.</q> <em>Race and Society</em>, 4(2), 161<ndash />176.
        Statistics South Africa (2023). <em>Census 2022: Statistical
        Release P0301.4.</em> Pretoria: Stats SA.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>
